🚽 Test case: round-24 review finding #1 -- current_return_value is one
🚽 global slot, and every function/native call overwrites it. handle_
🚽 return_statement() used to set current_return_value.type/.pointer_level
🚽 to the enclosing function's OWN declared return type before evaluating
🚽 the return expression, then write only current_return_value.value.*
🚽 afterward -- so if evaluating the expression invoked ANOTHER call (a
🚽 typed native here: takes_cstring(), which returns STDROT_INT), that
🚽 nested call's own completion overwrote current_return_value.type with
🚽 ITS type (VAR_INT), which nothing ever restored. The outer function
🚽 (declared gigachad/double) still correctly wrote the CONVERTED double
🚽 payload into current_return_value.value.dvalue -- but handle_function_
🚽 call()'s caller-side switch then read current_return_value.type (still
🚽 VAR_INT, stolen from the nested call) and consumed the wrong union
🚽 member entirely. Confirmed via a temporary A/B test (git stash to the
🚽 pre-fix commit) that this printed "0.000000" instead of "3.000000".
🚽 Fixed by evaluating into a local and only installing (type,
🚽 pointer_level, value) together, atomically, after evaluation
🚽 completes -- payload and metadata can no longer disagree about which
🚽 call they belong to.
gigachad widen() {
    bussin takes_cstring("abc");
}

skibidi main {
    gigachad x = widen();
    yappin("%f\n", x);
    bussin 0;
}
